    safetyLevel: "warning",
    body: "\u2022 Heart recipients (ISHLT, e65, Class I, C): for new-onset seizures not due to stroke or structural brain disease, management \"should include reduction of CNI doses (taking into consideration the risk of inadequate immunosuppression) and correction of hypomagnesemia, if present\". [7]\n\u2022 Heart recipients (e65, ungraded): for patients with epilepsy or needing antiepileptic drugs, \"In general, phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, and valproic acid should be avoided and fosphenytoin should be used with caution\". [7]\n\u2022 Tacrolimus labels (7.2): anticonvulsants \"(e.g., phenytoin, carbamazepine and phenobarbital)\" are strong CYP3A inducers that \"may decrease tacrolimus whole blood trough concentrations and increase the risk of rejection\"; cyclosporine labels list those three as decreasing cyclosporine concentrations. [1,2,3,4,5,6]\n\u2022 App content: Status Epilepticus (status-epilepticus.ts, node se-2nd-line-choice, line 331): \"Levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, and valproate had equivalent efficacy\"; node se-phenobarbital (line 466): \"Use when ESETT agents (levetiracetam, valproate, fosphenytoin) are unavailable\".\n\nEditor note: open [Status epilepticus](#/tree/status-epilepticus/node/se-2nd-line-choice) for general seizure steps.",
